  1	# DX12 agent rehearsal
  2	
  3	Use one records directory per release rehearsal. Build the published docs and pack the release candidate before running. The harness reads `apps/docs/dist` and `tests/dist/.packed/axylo-0.0.0.tgz`. Installations of Codex CLI, Claude Code, system Chrome, and the repository development dependencies are required. Codex uses the file login at `~/.codex/auth.json`. Claude uses `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` when nonempty and otherwise uses the machine's OAuth login at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.
  4	
  5	```sh
  6	node rehearsal/run.mjs --attempts 3 --parallel 2 --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal
  7	node rehearsal/summary.mjs --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal
  8	```
  9	
 10	## Release protocol
 11	
 12	- Use the fixed prompts for `daily-visits`, `regional-sales`, `signups`, and `daily-revenue`. Run three fresh, uncoached attempts per task with each of Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5`. Both request `xhigh` effort and have 20 minutes per attempt. Codex reports the observed model and effort through its rollout. Claude reports the observed model in assistant messages. The invocation records the requested `--effort` and the stream leaves effective effort unknown. An observed Claude version suffix is retained and must agree across counted runs for that agent.
 13	- Give each agent the same prompt bytes, published docs, installed package and primary data. Allow reading inputs, writing the answer and running documented Node self-checks within the time limit. Grade after the CLI exits. Keep grading data, expected results and grader output out of the staged inputs and give the agent no feedback or follow-up.
 14	- An invocation fills open slots up to `--attempts` per selected cell. The default is three. `--parallel` caps concurrent attempts and defaults to one. Omit `--agent` and `--task` to run all eight cells. Use one runner invocation at a time for a records directory. `--workdir` selects the scratch parent outside the repository and defaults to the system temporary directory. Records default to `rehearsal/records/`.
 15	- Keep passes, failures and timeouts as counted outcomes. Only voids and infrastructure failures leave slots open. A CLI authentication or provider failure or an exit before a model turn is an infrastructure failure. The runner records the reason and uses saved transcripts to identify older infrastructure failures. Rerun the same batch to fill open slots after inspecting the saved reason. The new record links to the record it replaces. A harness timeout still counts when truncation leaves the audit unverifiable and the transcript has no recorded path violation or evidence of a CLI infrastructure failure.
 16	- The owner decided on 2026-11-19 that the 1.0 rehearsal rule requires at least two passes in three counted slots in each of the eight agent × task cells. The summary shows the counts and rule status for each cell, the batch totals, and the recorded failures. It also checks that counted records use matching docs, package, task inputs, grader, tool versions and observed model IDs. Keep the release candidate and CLI installations fixed while collecting a batch. An empty summary displays zero counts for all cells.
 17	- Report this as “agent rehearsal: these prompts, these models, Chrome; not user evidence”. With three independent runs per cell, the two-sided exact 95% binomial interval is 9.43% to 99.16% for two successes and 29.24% to 100% for three. The counts apply to these line and bar tasks in Chrome and do not establish general success rates or human usability.
 18	
 19	## Isolation and audit
 20	
 21	Each attempt gets a newly created directory outside the repository with a fresh home, temporary storage and workspace. The workspace contains copies of the published docs, the unpacked package, primary data and prompt, and a writable location for `chart.js` and its helpers. The docs retain published examples and exclude internal source snapshots. The runner applies `chmod -R a-w` to `docs/` and `node_modules/` and removes write permission from the fixture, prompt and package manifest. These file modes protect against accidental writes. A sufficiently privileged or same-owner subprocess can still change modes or replace inputs. The runner verifies staged input hashes before grading, and the browser grader supplies canonical data from the harness.
 22	
 23	- **Codex CLI 0.170.0.** The runner assigns a fresh `CODEX_HOME` and symlinks only `auth.json` from the real login. It uses `--ignore-user-config` and `--ignore-rules`, disables apps, plugins, memory and hooks, skips host skill discovery, and disables web search. It uses `-s workspace-write`, `-a never`, and `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=false`. The CLI help describes `--sandbox` as selecting the sandbox for “model-generated shell commands” and says `--ignore-user-config` still uses `CODEX_HOME` for auth. Workspace-write applies write and network restrictions to agent commands. It allows host reads, and Codex's shell can run more than Node. The prompt and audit constrain use of that wider surface.
 24	- **Claude Code 2.1.327.** Each attempt has a fresh `HOME` and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`. With an API key, the runner passes it through and uses `--bare`, which skips OAuth, auto-memory and `CLAUDE.md` discovery. For OAuth the only initial config entry is a `.credentials.json` symlink into the real home. The runner creates and removes the symlink without opening the credential file. The fresh config and staged workspace contain no local Claude customizations or saved memory. Without `--bare`, `--safe-mode` disables `CLAUDE.md` discovery, skills, installed plugins, hooks and other customizations, and `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` turns off auto-memory. Built-in plugins, admin-managed settings and managed plugin hooks can still apply.
 25	- Both Claude modes keep `--restricted`, which ignores user, project and local settings and confines file tools to working directories. `--strict-mcp-config` limits MCP to the supplied empty configuration, `--disable-slash-commands` disables skills and commands, and `--no-session-persistence` turns off session saves in print mode. The built-in allowlist exposes file tools and Bash, with `--allowedTools` limiting Bash permission to `node …` and `dontAsk` denying other calls. `--permission-prompts none` denies anything that would prompt. Web tools and Chrome are absent. A Node subprocess can have host filesystem and network access through the operating system.
 26	- Both CLIs need their provider transport. These CLI controls give fresh session state and restrict configured tools. They do not provide a filesystem namespace or a transport-only network policy for Claude's Node subprocess. For strictly enforced docs-only reads and network isolation, use external isolation that enforces those boundaries for both CLIs. Keep host managed policies fixed and record them with the rehearsal if they apply.
 27	
 28	The audit requires a parseable transcript with the CLI's start and terminal events. Empty, malformed, or truncated transcripts are **unverifiable**. The audit takes paths from file-tool inputs and literal shell file operands, including redirects and paths relative to an explicit working directory or a `cd` command. It skips command bodies blocked by the CLI before execution, file contents, inline programs, search patterns, tool output and assistant prose. The audit can resolve symlinks when it has filesystem evidence. “No findings” describes the transcript only. A subprocess's actual reads, dynamically constructed paths and fetches from Node code are outside the transcript's coverage.
 29	
 30	The attempt's workspace, fresh home and config, and temporary storage are all inside its run directory. Recorded accesses to the repository, the real home, another attempt or the linked login void an attempt, even for missing files and after a timeout. System tools are allowed. Other outside paths are recorded under “looked outside the workspace,” with each path's existence. Only existing paths in that group void the attempt. Excluded tools also make a completed audit ineligible for grading.
 31	
 32	Each record stores `run.json` with the CLI version, arguments, requested and observed model metadata, elapsed time and status, and SHA-256 hashes of both inputs, their expected results, the prompt, copied docs, packed library and grader. Claude records include `claudeMode` as `bare` or `fresh-config`. The record also stores the transcript, stderr, answer files if present, and the grade and Chrome version if the answer reached Chrome. Audit version 2 records the original workspace and the filesystem observations used to resolve paths. The disposable workspace and home, including the credential symlink, are removed after saving the record. An interrupted batch may leave an incomplete record or scratch directory.
 33	
 34	## Reader grading and verification
 35	
 36	The grader uses clean headless Chrome contexts in Los Angeles and Tokyo with English (US) as the page locale. Each answer must handle the original and changed-value input. The alternate signups input has an absent value field that should produce an omission note. Dates are graded as calendar days, including unambiguous month-first and day-first forms. Series labels must match the requested fields. The fixtures declare no measurement unit or currency. The grader opens the standard table control, checks caption, semantic headers and rows, reads the plotted x order and SVG stroke breaks, samples the first calendar date through the plot control, and checks title clipping and omission counts in both the visible note and the accessibility tree.
 37	
 38	Re-auditing is allowed because the audit applies deterministic rules to the saved transcript and its path context. Version 1 records lack saved filesystem observations, so replay checks outside file existence on the host and can only resolve symlinks still present there. Run the command on the original host with the release candidate's built docs and packed library available. Use one writer at a time for the records directory.
 39	
 40	```sh
 41	node rehearsal/reaudit.mjs --records rehearsal/records/release-1e93128
 42	node rehearsal/summary.mjs --records rehearsal/records/release-1e93128
 43	```
 44	
 45	The command re-audits every record and uses the runner's grader for voids eligible for grading. It prepares a fresh workspace, verifies the input and grader hashes in `run.json`, then restores and grades the saved answer. A hash mismatch or a browser infrastructure error leaves the previous verdict in place and causes exit 2. The transcript and answer stay intact. Every updated record keeps the previous verdict, audit version and date in `run.json` under `history`. A re-audited record entering a counted slot keeps its original attempt number. The summary uses the latest verdicts and reports how many records were re-audited.
 46	
 47	For a single prepared workspace or the test suite, use the following commands.
 48	
 49	```sh
 50	node rehearsal/grade.mjs --task daily-visits --workdir /path/to/prepared/workspace
 51	npx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal --exclude '**/*.browser.test.mjs'
 52	npx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal
 53	```
 54	
 55	The first Vitest command runs fixture, comparator, audit and local-runner tests without Chrome. The second also runs the browser references and negative controls. The runner exits 0 when all newly scheduled attempts pass or there are no open slots, 1 for any new failed, timed-out or void attempt, and 2 for setup or infrastructure errors. Use the summary to evaluate the whole release rehearsal.
 56	
 57	## Daily revenue grammar input
 58	
 59	The grader also runs `daily-revenue.grammar.json` in both time zones. `"+2"` reads as `2`. `".5"` and `"0x10"` each leave a gap and count toward the reader's omission note. Both the input and the expected results are hashed into each daily-revenue record. The 1e93128 batch covered the primary and alternate inputs. Its separate grammar probe found converters that accepted `"0x10"` or `".5"`. [Rehearsal analysis](../reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-analysis.md)
